     3	\begin{document}
     4	
     5	\title{Secret Sharing  Based on a Hard-on-Average Problem}
     6	\date{}
     7	\author{P. Caballero-Gil and  C. Hern\'andez-Goya\\
     8	{\small DEIOC, University of La Laguna, 38271 La Laguna, Tenerife, Spain.} \\
     9	{\small pcaballe@ull.es, mchgoya@ull.es}}
    10	\maketitle
    11	\begin{abstract}
    12	
    13	The main goal of this work is to propose the design of secret
    14	sharing schemes based on hard-on-average problems. It includes the
    15	description of a new multiparty protocol  whose main application
    16	is key management in  networks. Its unconditionally perfect
    17	security relies on a discrete mathematics problem classified as
    18	DistNP-Complete under the average-case analysis, the so-called
    19	\emph{Distributional Matrix Representability Problem}. Thanks to
    20	the use of the search version of the mentioned decision problem,
    21	the security of the proposed scheme is  guaranteed. Although
    22	several secret sharing schemes connected with combinatorial
    23	structures may be found in the bibliography, the main contribution
    24	of this work is the proposal of a new secret sharing scheme based
    25	on a hard-on-average problem, which allows to enlarge the set of
    26	tools for designing more secure cryptographic applications.
    27	
    28	
    29	Keywords: Cryptography, Secret Sharing, Matrices,
    30	Computational Complexity.
    31	
    32	AMS classifications: 94A60, 94A62.
    33	
    34	\end{abstract}
    35	
    36	\section{Introduction}\label{intro}
    37	
    38	\footnotetext{Research supported by the Spanish Ministry of Education and
    39	Science and the European FEDER Fund under Project
    40	SEG2004-04352-C04-03.\\
    41	Linear Algebra and its Applications. Volume 414, Issues 2-3, 15 April 2006, Pages 626-631 \\
    42	DOI: 10.1016/j.laa.2005.10.040 
    43	}
    44	
    45	When a group of nodes in an open network wants to use standard
    46	security services such as authentication, confidentiality or data
    47	integrity, they usually have to share a common secret piece of
    48	information called session key.  In order to build a highly secure
    49	key management service in wireless ad hoc networks, threshold
    50	cryptography based on secret sharing schemes has been yet proposed
    51	in the literature \cite{ZH99}. Since the standard approach of
    52	replication is not suitable for these particularly vulnerable
    53	networks, the distribution of trust among the set of nodes seems
    54	to be the most robust solution. In this work, a new secret sharing
    55	scheme is proposed in order to increase the security level of
    56	known secret sharing schemes by basing them on hard-on-average
    57	problems.
    58	
    59	When choosing a specific problem as basis of a cryptographic
    60	application, one of the most important characteristics we have to
    61	look for is that, on the one hand, finding a solution must not be
    62	computationally feasible, whereas on the other hand, generating
    63	pairs formed by (instance, solution) can be efficiently
    64	accomplished. Another usual property of the selected problem is
    65	that the verification procedure for any solution should be as
    66	simple as possible.
    67	
    68	The  previous reasons justify the widespread use of problems
    69	belonging to the worst-case $NP$ and $NP - complete$ classes in
    70	the area of the design of cryptographic applications. However,
    71	NP-completeness only guarantees that there is no polynomial-time
    72	algorithm to solve a problem in the worst case. Furthermore, with
    73	the development of the Computational Complexity Theory, and
    74	concretely thanks to the advances made on the Average-Case
    75	analysis, it has been proved that many NP-complete problems may be
    76	efficiently solved when the instance is randomly generated
    77	\cite{Kar76}. So, our proposal to solve this problem is to choose
    78	as base problems those whose difficulty is guaranteed by the
    79	average-case analysis. In this paper, the first (to the knowledge
    80	of the authors) secret sharing scheme based on a problem
    81	catalogued as NP-complete from the point of view of the
    82	average-case analysis is proposed. The problem that has been
    83	chosen as base for the protocol proposed here is the so-called
    84	\emph{Distributional Matrix Representability Problem}
    85	\cite{Wan97}, which is hard-on-average.
    86	
    87	The structure of the present work is as follows. The next section
    88	discusses the underlying problem in the framework of average-case
    89	complexity whereas Section 3 locates the proposal in the existing
    90	context of Secret Sharing. The proposed scheme is described in
    91	detail in section 4. Finally, several conclusions and open
    92	problems close the paper.
    93	
    94	%
    95	\section{The Underlying Problem}
    96	
    97	The average-case analysis is based on the concept of distributional decision problem
    98	\cite{Lev86}, which is formed by a decision problem and a probability distribution
    99	defined on the set of instances. In this context, the choice of the probability
   100	distribution plays an important role since to fix a probability distribution could
   101	directly influence the practical complexity of the problem. In fact, it has been
   102	proved the existence of NP-complete problems solvable in polynomial time when the
   103	instances are randomly generated under certain distributions.
   104	
   105	The distributional class analogous to NP in the hierarchy
   106	associated to the average-case analysis is the DistNP class. It is
   107	formed by pairs containing a decision problem belonging to the NP
   108	class and a  polynomially computable probability distribution. As
   109	in worst-case complexity theory, a distributional problem is said
   110	to be average-case NP-complete (or DistNP-complete) if it is in
   111	DistNP and every distributional problem in DistNP is reducible to
   112	it. The first problem catalogued as DistNP-complete was the
   113	distributional tiling problem, whose formal proof of membership
   114	may been found in \cite{Lev86}. Later, a few works containing the
   115	description of new average-case NP-complete problems have been
   116	published \cite{VR92}, \cite{VL88}, \cite{Gur90},  \cite{Wan97}.
   117	
   118	The main difficulty when trying to use problems belonging to this
   119	category in practical applications is the artificiality of their
   120	specifications. So, the principal reason why we have chosen the
   121	Distributional Matrix Representability Problem as base for the
   122	Secret Sharing Scheme here proposed is its naive formulation.
   123	
   124	The original Distributional Matrix Representability Problem can be
   125	roughly defined in the following way. All the matrices in the
   126	problem are square and with integer entries. Given an $r \times r$
   127	matrix $A$ and a set of matrices with the same size $\{ A_{1}$,
   128	$A_{2}, ..., A_{k}\}$, it should be decided whether $A$ can be
   129	expressed as a product of matrices in the given set.
   130	
   131	In the present work  a bounded version of such a problem for $20
   132	\times 20$ matrices is proposed to be used. In this case the
   133	instance consists of a matrix $A$, a set of $k$ distinct matrices
   134	$\{ A_{1}$, $A_{2}, ..., A_{k}\}$ and a positive integer $n \leq k
   135	$, and the question to answer may be stated as follows: is it
   136	possible to express $A$ as a product of $n$ matrices belonging to
   137	the set $\{ A_{1}$, $A_{2}, ..., A_{k}\}$.
   138	 This bounded version of the
   139	problem was shown to be average-case NP-complete by Venkatesan and Rajagopalan
   140	\cite{VR92}. The distribution considered to generate the integers $k$ and $n$ and the
   141	integer entries of the matrices is the uniform distribution.
   142	
   143	The proposed scheme uses the search version of the above
   144	distributional problem, which consists in finding $n$ matrices in
   145	$\{ A_{1}$, $A_{2}, ..., A_{k}\}$ whose product is $A$. The
   146	difficulty of such a version is equivalent to that of the original
   147	distributional decision problem as may be deduced from the general
   148	result stated in \cite{BCGL92}, according to which, search and
   149	decision distributional problems are equivalent from the
   150	average-case analysis point of view.
   151	
   152	%
   153	\section{Secret Sharing Schemes}
   154	%
   155	Secret sharing multiparty protocols solve usual practical
   156	situations where it is necessary the distribution of a particular
   157	secret $A$ among a set of users $M$. Such a context may be
   158	illustrated with the problem of secret key management. The main
   159	objective of Secret Sharing Schemes is to guarantee that only
   160	pre-designated subsets of participants are able to reconstruct the
   161	secret by collectively combining their shares (or shadows) of $A$.
   162	The specification of all the subsets of participants which are
   163	authorized to recreate the secret is called the access structure
   164	of the Secret Sharing Scheme. An access structure is said to be
   165	monotone if any set which contains a subset that can recover the
   166	secret can itself recover the secret. A general methodology to
   167	design Secret Sharing Schemes for arbitrary monotone access
   168	structure was given in  \cite{c88-Benaloh-Leichter} and
   169	\cite{Ito:Sai:Nis87} . However, such results were not useful for
   170	the Secret Sharing Scheme proposed here because the access
   171	structure is not monotone.
   172	
   173	The first secret sharing schemes were independently proposed in
   174	1979 \cite{bla79}, \cite{Sha79}. Later it was demonstrated that
   175	both proposals can be considered described in a common general
   176	scheme due to their basis on the same principles of linear
   177	algebra, \cite{c84-Kothari}. Many different mathematical
   178	structures such as polynomials, geometric configurations, block
   179	designs, Reed-Solomon codes, vector spaces, matroids, complete
   180	multipartite graphs, orthogonal arrays and Latin squares have been
   181	used to model secret sharing schemes. The scheme here described is
   182	based on the generation of a secret matrix as product of matrices
   183	with the same size. Another scheme  based also in matrices was
   184	proposed in \cite{Kar:Gre:Hel83}, but there the secret is a
   185	solution to a system of linear equations.
   186	
   187	In general, the structure of most Secret Sharing Schemes is based
   188	on two phases. In the initialization phase, a third trusted party
   189	called the dealer, distributes shares of the secret to authorized
   190	participants through a secure channel. In the reconstruction
   191	phase, the authorized participants of a subset in the access
   192	structure combine their shares to reconstruct the secret. In the
   193	initialization of the Secret Sharing Scheme proposed here the
   194	dealer will publish all the shares and the only secret information
   195	that is revealed to each participant is a pointer to a concrete
   196	share and the names of the other parties in the same access
   197	structure.
   198	
   199	Secret sharing schemes which do not reveal any information about
   200	the shared secret to unauthorized individuals are called perfect.
   201	On the other hand, a concept extensively used in Secret Sharing
   202	Schemes is the unconditional security of perfect schemes. A Secret
   203	Sharing Scheme is considered unconditionally secure against
   204	cheaters if the probability of successfully cheating does not
   205	depend on the computational abilities of the cheaters.  The Secret
   206	Sharing Scheme proposed in the following Section is perfect and
   207	unconditionally secure.
   208	
   209	\section{A Secret Sharing Scheme Based on an Average-Case Intractable Problem}
   210	
   211	In the following secret sharing scheme,  the participation of a
   212	Third Trusted Party is only necessary during the first off-line
   213	initialization stage of the generation of the secret.
   214	
   215	Since within the protocol many products of matrices have to be
   216	carried out, it is advisable that all those products are carried
   217	out using the algorithm proposed in \cite{Cop:Win87} due to its
   218	efficiency. On the other hand, the Monte Carlo algorithms
   219	described by Freivalds \cite{Fre79} for the verification of the
   220	product of two matrices may be used to achieve the fraud detection
   221	process. The error probability in these algorithms is bounded by
   222	$2^{-t}$, where $t$ is the number of iterations to be performed.
   223	
   224	In order to simplify the general description of the protocol it
   225	will be split into two phases: Initialization and Reconstruction.
   226	%
   227	\subsection{Initialization}
   228	%
   229	This set-up stage consists of the generation of the secret, task
   230	that is equivalent to the generation of an instance of the
   231	underlying problem. It should be carried out privately by the
   232	Third Trusted Party.
   233	
   234	It starts with the random generation of two integers $k$ and $n$
   235	such that $n \leq k$,
   236	 where $n$ indicates the number of participants.
   237	
   238	Then, $k$ $20\times 20$ matrices $A_{i}, i = 1, \ldots, k$ with
   239	integer entries  are randomly generated. All the matrices form a
   240	public set denoted by $M$ and are identified by  indices of their
   241	positions in this set. An
   242	 ordered
   243	 subset of $n$ matrices
   244	constitutes the access structure and their  product, $A$, is
   245	 the secret information.
   246	
   247	In this phase  the existence of a secure communication
   248	 channel is necessary because
   249	 each authorized participant has to learn from the Third Trusted Party which is his or her secret shadow in
   250	 $M$, and who is his or her neighbor in the product ordering.
   251	The  main problem of this stage is usually the bandwidth necessary
   252	to transfer the shadows. However, such a difficulty is here
   253	avoided by sending to each authorized participant user only  the
   254	index pointing to the corresponding matrix in the access
   255	structure.
   256	
   257	Finally,  the Third Trusted Party sends to the i-th participant a
   258	random binary vector $U_i$ with Hamming weight greater than 1 and
   259	the same dimension that the matrices intervening in the protocol.
   260	The corresponding product vectors between these random vectors and
   261	the secret matrix $A$, $U'_i=A\cdot U_i$, are then published. In
   262	this way, each pair of vectors $U_i$ and $U'_i$ is only available
   263	to  i-th participant, who cannot obtain the secret $A$ from such
   264	information since both vectors define a system of 20 linear
   265	equations in 400 unknowns.
   266	
   267	
   268	\subsection{Reconstruction} This stage  begins with a verification that allows the following two things: Detect the
   269	presence of cheaters among the shadow holders, and guarantee the
   270	correctness of the secret construction.
   271	
   272	
   273	In order to allow the verification step, the $n$ different
   274	circular shifts of the original order  of the participants set
   275	$\{P_{(i)}, P_{(i+1)}, \ldots, P_{(i+n-1)}\}$ (where all the
   276	sub-indices are $(mod$ $ (n+1)$) are considered. Such shifts
   277	establish the order in which the verification is developed. The
   278	participant designated in first place according to the shift
   279	($P_{(i)}$) computes
   280	 the product between his or her shadow and the random
   281	binary vector $U_i$ obtaining $A_{(i)}U_i$, then he or she sends
   282	the obtained result to the next participant ($P_{(i+1)}$)
   283	determined by the permutation. In this way, $P_{(i+1)}$ computes
   284	the product determined by his or her shadow and the vector
   285	provided by $P_{(i)}$, $(A_{(i+1)}A_{(i)}U_i)$, and so on. Only if
   286	all the participants have been honest, the last participant
   287	obtains $U'_i$. After comparing it with the published vector, he
   288	or she communicates  the result of the verification to the others.
   289	According to this process, if some participant tries to forge his
   290	or her shadow, the forgery is always detected by the last
   291	participant.
   292	
   293	Once passed the verification stage, according to the same
   294	permutations as before, each first participant $P_i$ has to choose
   295	randomly a secret integer $20\times 20$ matrix $X_i$ and to reveal
   296	publicly the product between his or her shadow and $ X_i$ to the
   297	following user.
   298	     The receiver multiplies his or her shadow by the received information
   299	      and sends the result to the following user,
   300	    and
   301	    so on. Finally, the last participant has to give what he or she received to the first user, who will be able to
   302	        recover the secret from $A_{(i-1)}A_{(i-2)}\cdots A_1A_nA_{n-1}\cdots A_i X_i$. In order to do it,
   303	        he or she has to multiply such a matrix by $(A_nA_{n-1}\cdots A_i X_i)^{-1}$ by the right,
   304	        and by
   305	         $(A_nA_{n-1}\cdots A_i X_i)(X_i)^{-1}$   by the left.
   306	\subsection{Comments}
   307	
   308	The main parameter of secret sharing schemes is the number of
   309	necessary participants to recover the secret, known as the
   310	cardinality of the privilege users in the access structure. In the
   311	proposed scheme, such a number, denoted by $n$, must be large
   312	enough to avoid that an exhaustive search attack may be
   313	successful. With respect to this point, note that in the original
   314	description of the scheme it is assumed that $n<$20 so that the
   315	participants cannot find $A$ without executing the algorithm. In
   316	general, for any number of participants $n$,  the size of matrices
   317	should be $r>n$ in order to guarantee the security of the scheme.
   318	
   319	On the other hand, since the search space for the exhaustive
   320	search is formed by all possible products of $n$ matrices in the
   321	set $M$, and has cardinality $\left(
   322	\begin{array}{c}k+n-1 \\ n
   323	\end{array}\right)$, the number $k$ should also be well chosen.
   324	 The integer $x$ is another important parameter that should be randomly selected in order to
   325	 guarantee the security of the system.
   326	
   327	Within the generation of the vector $U$ the binary vectors with
   328	Hamming weight $1$ should be discarded, because otherwise the
   329	vector $U'$ will coincide with a column of the secret matrix $A$.
   330	Thus, the cardinality of the set of possible binary vectors is
   331	$2^{20}-20$.
   332	
   333	The security of the proposed scheme is guaranteed by the
   334	difficulty of the distributional matrix representability problem
   335	because any unauthorized individuals only know the public set of
   336	$k$ matrices $\{ A_{1}$, $A_{2}, ..., A_{k}\}$ and a positive
   337	integer $n \leq k $, and from that knowledge they cannot guess
   338	which is the subset that produces the secret $A$. On the other
   339	hand, if the size of matrices is $r>n$, any group of less than $n$
   340	authorized individuals only know a part of the subset that
   341	produces $A$, and have not enough information to find the
   342	remaining matrices.
   343	
   344	     The described procedure allows to carry
   345	    out a secure multiparty computation of the secret information without revealing any valuable information about
   346	the shared secret to unauthorized individuals, so the scheme is
   347	perfect. Also, the proposed scheme is unconditionally secure
   348	because the probability of successfully cheating does not depend
   349	on the computational abilities of the cheaters. On the other hand,
   350	the distribution
   351	    of computation  among all the participants is balanced, which turn them into  ideal candidates as primitive tools for
   352	    designing  key management schemes in wireless ad hoc networks.
   353	
   354	\section{Conclusions}
   355	In this paper we have proposed a secret sharing scheme based on an
   356	average-case NP-complete problem, the so-called distributional
   357	matrix representability problem. The proposal does not reveal any
   358	valuable  information about the shared secret matrix to
   359	unauthorized parties, and the size of each share equals the size
   360	of the secret, so the scheme is ideal. Also, since the
   361	distribution
   362	    of computation  among all the participants is balanced, the proposal seems a useful primitive for defining  a key management
   363	    protocol in wireless ad hoc networks.    The study of concrete
   364	constructions of difficult instances of the problem that are
   365	adequate according to the design of the scheme is part of a work
   366	in progress.
