codex
Implemented and verified a native Track B generator based on the paper’s ordered integer-matrix representation problem ([arXiv:1005.0054](https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.0054)).

Key results:

- Shipping preset: `easy`, with 20×20 matrices, 24 factors, and a 10-index witness.
- All G1–G9 gates pass.
- Random guessing: 0/200,000 over `24P10`.
- Four adversarial attacks: each 0/8.
- Reference algorithm: 8/8, 156,813 scalar operations.
- Compact invariant route: 29 exact operations.
- Oracle arms: bare 0/3, hinted 0/3, placebo 0/3.
- Canonical key: 140/140 relabelling and unimodular-conjugation checks.

Files:

- [gen_1005_0054.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/gen_1005_0054.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1005.0054/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The documentation explicitly avoids a false Track A claim: the paper’s flat hard-on-average distribution does not cover its dealer-generated planted yes-instances. It also records and resolves the paper’s conflict between “ordered subset” and its later multiset search-space formula.
